12:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1a",null,{"guide":{"date":"March 11–April 3, 1862; preparations for the Battle of Shiloh","place":"Savannah, Crump’s Landing, Pittsburg Landing, and Corinth along the Tennessee River corridor","result":"Grant’s army established dispersed camps at Pittsburg Landing and Crump’s Landing while waiting for Buell. The United States position was organized for an advance on Corinth, not for defense. Johnston ordered about 42,000 Confederates north from Corinth, but delays disrupted the timetable. The written deployment order stacked four corps one behind another instead of assigning them separate sectors, creating a command problem before the battle began","facts":[{"label":"Confederate strength at Corinth","value":"About 42,000 troops"},{"label":"Corinth to Pittsburg Landing","value":"About 23 road miles"},{"label":"United States transport fleet","value":"About 80 steamboats assembled at Savannah"},{"label":"Grant’s main position","value":"Five divisions near Pittsburg Landing; Lew Wallace at Crump’s Landing"},{"label":"Original Confederate timetable","value":"Leave Corinth April 3 and attack at dawn April 4"},{"label":"Confederate corps","value":"Polk 9,100; Bragg 13,500; Hardee 6,700; Breckinridge 6,400"},{"label":"Johnston’s intended attack","value":"Bragg on the right, Hardee in the center, Polk on the left"},{"label":"Order actually issued","value":"Hardee, Bragg, Polk, and Breckinridge in successive lines across the battlefield"}],"maps":[{"title":"Grant’s river bases and the Confederate concentration","caption":"Savannah was on the east bank of the Tennessee River. Crump’s Landing and Pittsburg Landing were on the west bank. Corinth lay about 23 road miles southwest of the main camps.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-89.05%2C34.70%2C-87.95%2C35.45&layer=mapnik&marker=35.1488%2C-88.3228"},{"title":"The movement toward Pittsburg Landing","caption":"Grant’s army came up the Tennessee River, Buell approached Savannah from Nashville, and Johnston prepared to move north from Corinth before they could unite.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/shiloh-history.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-campaign-approach.svg","alt":"Strategic diagram of the armies converging on Pittsburg Landing and Savannah"},{"title":"Johnston’s concept and Jordan’s deployment order","caption":"Johnston described corps operating as left, center, and right, with Bragg driving the United States left away from the river. Thomas Jordan’s written order instead placed entire corps in successive lines, making intermingling likely once the front line stopped.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/shiloh-history.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-confederate-deployment.svg","alt":"Comparison of Johnston’s intended three-wing attack with the four stacked Confederate corps lines ordered for Shiloh"},{"title":"Camps and terrain at Shiloh","caption":"The NPS map identifies Shiloh Church, Pittsburg Landing, the major fields, roads, ravines, creeks, and the separation between Lew Wallace and the five divisions near the landing.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/planyourvisit/maps.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-nps-auto-tour-map.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of the Shiloh battlefield and tour route"},{"title":"Historical map of Shiloh","caption":"The 1862 map approved by Beauregard shows the camps, roads, landmarks, army positions, and the Tennessee River.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/85690890/","image":"/images/shiloh-beauregard-map.jpg","alt":"1862 map of the Shiloh battlefield approved by P.G.T. Beauregard"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:08","audioTime":68,"title":"Johnston faced two approaching armies","detail":"About 42,000 Confederates at Corinth could fight Grant on roughly equal terms, but Grant and Buell together would have a large numerical advantage."},{"time":"3:12","audioTime":192,"title":"Johnston chose to defeat the armies separately","detail":"He planned to attack Grant before Buell completed the march from Nashville."},{"time":"3:51","audioTime":231,"title":"Halleck directed Grant and Buell","detail":"Halleck remained in St. Louis and intended to take field command after the two armies united."},{"time":"5:07","audioTime":307,"title":"C.F. Smith led the initial river movement","detail":"While Grant was sidelined, Smith took the army up the Tennessee River and Buell began marching from Nashville."},{"time":"5:31","audioTime":331,"title":"The first troops reached Savannah","detail":"The 46th Ohio arrived March 11. About eighty steamboats brought the rest of Smith’s army over the following days."},{"time":"6:17","audioTime":377,"title":"Smith assigned railroad raids","detail":"Sherman was to cut the Memphis & Charleston Railroad, and Lew Wallace was to damage the Mobile & Ohio Railroad near Purdy."},{"time":"6:54","audioTime":414,"title":"C.F. Smith injured his leg","detail":"Smith scraped his shin while boarding a launch. The wound became infected and eventually caused his death."},{"time":"7:18","audioTime":438,"title":"Lew Wallace completed his raid","detail":"His division marched from Crump’s Landing and destroyed a half-mile trestle north of Purdy, then occupied positions from Adamsville to the river."},{"time":"8:07","audioTime":487,"title":"Sherman moved upriver","detail":"Nineteen transports carried his division toward Eastport to reach the Memphis & Charleston Railroad."},{"time":"8:21","audioTime":501,"title":"Confederates blocked the Eastport landing","detail":"Sherman shifted to Yellow Creek, but heavy rain, mud, and an unfordable creek ended the attempt."},{"time":"9:08","audioTime":548,"title":"Hurlbut reached Pittsburg Landing","detail":"Sherman encountered Hurlbut’s division unloading at the river bluff about eight miles above Savannah."},{"time":"9:39","audioTime":579,"title":"Smith selected Pittsburg Landing as the camp","detail":"He ordered Sherman to camp several miles inland, leaving room for later divisions between Sherman and the river."},{"time":"10:07","audioTime":607,"title":"Sherman occupied Shiloh Church","detail":"His four brigades camped about two and a half miles from the river, with headquarters at the log Methodist meetinghouse."},{"time":"10:25","audioTime":625,"title":"The main army assembled","detail":"W.H.L. Wallace’s and McClernand’s divisions joined Hurlbut behind Sherman. Lew Wallace remained several miles away at Crump’s Landing."},{"time":"11:27","audioTime":687,"title":"Grant resumed command","detail":"Grant arrived at Savannah on March 17 after Halleck restored him to duty."},{"time":"12:54","audioTime":774,"title":"Smith’s condition worsened","detail":"The infected leg wound left him unable to command. He died at Savannah on April 25."},{"time":"13:20","audioTime":800,"title":"Halleck prohibited an advance","detail":"Grant wanted to move against Corinth, but orders required him to wait for Buell and avoid bringing on a battle."},{"time":"15:16","audioTime":916,"title":"The camps were not arranged for defense","detail":"The army expected to advance from Pittsburg Landing and did not anticipate a Confederate attack from Corinth."},{"time":"17:32","audioTime":1052,"title":"Buell crossed the Duck River","detail":"On April 2, his army cleared a major obstacle but still had about eighty miles to reach Grant."},{"time":"17:53","audioTime":1073,"title":"A cavalry probe alarmed Beauregard","detail":"Routine United States activity near Crump’s Landing was mistaken for the start of an offensive."},{"time":"18:36","audioTime":1116,"title":"Johnston ordered the army to march","detail":"The April 2 order called for departure at 6 a.m. on April 3, arrival near Pittsburg Landing that day, and an attack at dawn April 4."},{"time":"24:03","audioTime":1443,"title":"The march began hours late","detail":"Inexperienced units, inadequate staff work, and unclear orders kept most troops in camp. Hardee finally left Corinth at 3 p.m."},{"time":"25:12","audioTime":1512,"title":"The attack was postponed","detail":"Johnston, Beauregard, and Bragg rescheduled the assault from Friday to Saturday morning."},{"time":"25:21","audioTime":1521,"title":"Thomas Jordan wrote the detailed order","detail":"Beauregard’s chief of staff prepared a complex march and deployment plan for four unequal corps."},{"time":"26:48","audioTime":1608,"title":"The corps were stacked in successive lines","detail":"Hardee formed the first line, followed by Bragg and Polk, with Breckinridge in reserve. Each line stretched across the battlefield."},{"time":"27:26","audioTime":1646,"title":"The order differed from Johnston’s stated plan","detail":"Johnston had described Polk on the left, Hardee in the center, and Bragg on the right driving the United States left away from the river."},{"time":"28:22","audioTime":1702,"title":"The deployment would break corps control","detail":"Successive formations would feed into the same contested ground and intermingle, reducing the corps to collections of brigades and regiments."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Henry W. Halleck","command":"Department of the Mississippi","subordinates":"Ordered Grant to wait for Buell and avoid a battle"},{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant","command":"Army of the Tennessee","subordinates":"Headquarters at Savannah; five divisions near Pittsburg Landing and one at Crump’s Landing"},{"commander":"William T. Sherman","command":"5th Division","subordinates":"Forward camps around Shiloh Church"},{"commander":"Stephen A. Hurlbut","command":"4th Division","subordinates":"Camps behind Sherman toward Pittsburg Landing"},{"commander":"W.H.L. Wallace","command":"2nd Division","subordinates":"Succeeded the injured C.F. Smith"},{"commander":"John A. McClernand","command":"1st Division","subordinates":"Camps behind Sherman’s forward division"},{"commander":"Lew Wallace","command":"3rd Division","subordinates":"Separated from the main army at Crump’s Landing and nearby roads"},{"commander":"Don Carlos Buell","command":"Army of the Ohio","subordinates":"Marching from Nashville toward Savannah"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Albert Sidney Johnston","command":"Army of the Mississippi","subordinates":"Army commander; ordered the march and attack"},{"commander":"P.G.T. Beauregard","command":"Second in command","subordinates":"Organized the army and assigned Thomas Jordan to draft the written order"},{"commander":"Leonidas Polk","command":"1st Corps","subordinates":"About 9,100 men; third line in the written deployment"},{"commander":"Braxton Bragg","command":"2nd Corps","subordinates":"About 13,500 men; second line in the written deployment"},{"commander":"William J. Hardee","command":"3rd Corps","subordinates":"About 6,700 men; first line and advance guard"},{"commander":"John C. Breckinridge","command":"Reserve Corps","subordinates":"About 6,400 men following the three main lines"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Albert Sidney Johnston","role":"Ordered the march from Corinth and accepted the faulty deployment arrangement","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/albert-sidney-johnston.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018670318/"},{"name":"P.G.T. Beauregard","role":"Organized the four corps and delegated the detailed march order to his chief of staff","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Thomas Jordan","role":"Beauregard’s chief of staff who drafted the stacked-corps deployment order","side":"Confederacy"},{"personId":"ulysses-s-grant","role":"Resumed command at Savannah and kept the army in its exposed camps under Halleck’s orders"},{"personId":"charles-f-smith","role":"Selected the Savannah and Pittsburg Landing bases before an infected leg wound removed him from command"},{"name":"William T. Sherman","role":"Led the failed railroad raid and placed the forward division around Shiloh Church","side":"United States","image":"/images/william-t-sherman.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018670507/"},{"name":"Lew Wallace","role":"Cut the Mobile & Ohio trestle and remained with his division near Crump’s Landing","side":"United States"},{"name":"Braxton Bragg","role":"Commanded the largest Confederate corps and was assigned the second battle line","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"William J. Hardee","role":"Commanded the first Confederate line and led the delayed departure from Corinth","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Leonidas Polk","role":"Commanded the third Confederate line after earlier abandoning Columbus","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"John C. Breckinridge","role":"Commanded the Confederate reserve corps","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"Major General Charles F. Smith","side":"United States","role":"Temporary commander of Grant’s army and organizer of the river movement","outcome":"Died at Savannah on April 25 from infection after injuring his leg; he did not fight at Shiloh"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/pittsburg-landing-1862.jpg","alt":"Pittsburg Landing on the Tennessee River in 1862","caption":"Pittsburg Landing served as the unloading point and supply link for Grant’s camps on the bluff.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2004660985/"},{"image":"/images/shiloh-battlefield-april-9.jpg","alt":"Photograph of the Shiloh battlefield on April 9, 1862","caption":"A photographer recorded the battlefield two days after the fighting ended.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2017646904/"},{"image":"/images/shiloh-battle-print.jpg","alt":"Historical print of the Battle of Shiloh","caption":"This later print depicts the dense woods, broken terrain, and close fighting described in the episode.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018757088/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 112","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.libsyn.com/112-shiloh-part-the-second"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of Shiloh","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/shiloh-history.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Shiloh maps","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/planyourvisit/maps.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Shiloh battlefield map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/85690890/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Pittsburg Landing","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2004660985/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Shiloh battlefield, April 9","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2017646904/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Battle of Shiloh print","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018757088/"}]}}]}]
